<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/advanced/heat-treatment">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Heat treatment is <strong>microstructure by itinerary</strong>: same chemistry, different
    temperature–time path, completely different material. The blacksmith\'s hammer-hiss was this
    lesson in empirical form; the diagram lesson gave it a map, and modern metallurgy turned the
    oven into a precision instrument.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="quench, temper, anneal, case-harden" widget="equationStep" config={heatCfg} />

  <h2>The craft, modernised</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Why Samurai swords are materials science:</strong> folded steel with a clay-coated differential quench — hard edge, tough spine, visible hamon line: the quench-temper compromise discovered centuries before the diagram explained it (the metallurgy lesson\'s history).</li>
    <li><strong>Distortion and cracking are the costs:</strong> quenches are violent (thermal shock — the Ashby lesson\'s thermal-stress index); salts, oils and polymers moderate cooling curves; press-quenching jigs hold shapes. Half of heat-treatment engineering is managing the violence of the other half.</li>
    <li><strong>Precipitation hardening — the aerospace trick:</strong> alloying elements dissolved at high temperature, then aged at low temperature to precipitate nano-particles that pin dislocations — aluminium and superalloys gain strength from scheduled nano-precipitation (the defect lesson, weaponised).</li>
    <li><strong>Additive manufacturing rewrites the recipes:</strong> 3-D-printed metals undergo their own micro-welding thermal cycles — treating the whole part (hot isostatic pressing, stress relief) is the frontier: the map lesson\'s itineraries applied to a printing process.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Callister; ASM Handbook Vol. 4 (Heat Treating); the phase-diagram lesson of this ladder.</p>
</TopicPage>
